ready: 1
kind: rule
id: appraisal.rule.label
display_id: RULE-APPRAISAL-LABEL
module: appraisal
title: The functional emotion of an episode
summary: Names the appraisal pattern with one label, first match wins; consolidation files the episode by this label.
inputs:
  - name: appraisal
    source: "The episode's appraisal values, and the judge's misleading sources when it was judged"
    source_kind: derived
constants:
  - appraisal.const.thresholds
decision:
  nodes:
    - {id: in, node_kind: input, text: The episode's appraisal}
    - {id: d1, node_kind: decision, text: "Did the judge name a misleading source, and is every named event third-party?"}
    - {id: d2, node_kind: decision, text: Are the stakes at or above the alarm minimum and the valence at or below −0.3?}
    - {id: d3, node_kind: decision, text: "Is the social signal at or below −0.5, or is there a directive?"}
    - {id: d4, node_kind: decision, text: "Is the valence at or below −0.3, attributed to the agent, and either recurring or effortful?"}
    - {id: d5, node_kind: decision, text: Is the valence at or above 0.3 with effort at or above 0.6?}
    - {id: d6, node_kind: decision, text: Is the surprise at or above the surprise minimum?}
    - {id: d7, node_kind: decision, text: Is the social signal at or above 0.5?}
    - {id: d8, node_kind: decision, text: Is there an open loop?}
    - {id: l1, node_kind: outcome, text: distrust}
    - {id: l2, node_kind: outcome, text: alarm}
    - {id: l3, node_kind: outcome, text: correction}
    - {id: l4, node_kind: outcome, text: frustration}
    - {id: l5, node_kind: outcome, text: relief}
    - {id: l6, node_kind: outcome, text: surprise}
    - {id: l7, node_kind: outcome, text: approval}
    - {id: l8, node_kind: outcome, text: curiosity}
    - {id: l9, node_kind: outcome, text: "boredom, when nothing else matches"}
  edges:
    - {from: in, to: d1}
    - {from: d1, to: l1, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d1, to: d2, label: "No"}
    - {from: d2, to: l2, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d2, to: d3, label: "No"}
    - {from: d3, to: l3, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d3, to: d4, label: "No"}
    - {from: d4, to: l4, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d4, to: d5, label: "No"}
    - {from: d5, to: l5, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d5, to: d6, label: "No"}
    - {from: d6, to: l6, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d6, to: d7, label: "No"}
    - {from: d7, to: l7, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d7, to: d8, label: "No"}
    - {from: d8, to: l8, label: "Yes"}
    - {from: d8, to: l9, label: "No"}
default: boredom
rationale: >-
  Alarm comes before correction so a high-stakes failure that was also corrected becomes a
  pre-action guardrail, the stronger protection. Frustration needs agent attribution so a flaky
  network does not teach false lessons about the agent's approach. Distrust needs every misleading
  event to be third-party so Kernle never learns to distrust the person or the agent's own
  observations. Boredom is the explicit no-match outcome, so routine work reinforces what is known
  instead of creating new memories.
